    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ('COG', 'digital_infrastructure', 'Digital Infrastructure',
      $$Congo's digital infrastructure features mobile network coverage, Brazzaville-Pointe-Noire fiber, submarine cable access, and digital services supporting oil economy connectivity.$$,
      $$Republic of Congo (Brazzaville) has mobile telecommunications serving urban areas and expanding in rural zones. Fiber backbone connects Brazzaville and Pointe-Noire. Atlantic submarine cable connectivity provides international bandwidth. Internet access is growing. E-government services are developing. ICT sector serves oil industry, government, and urban population. Congo River positioning offers regional connectivity potential. The sector represents opportunity for mobile broadband expansion, fiber infrastructure extension, digital public services, fintech development, and technology adoption supporting economic diversification, oil sector digitalization, government efficiency, and regional connectivity in a French-speaking, oil-producing, Congo Basin country with Atlantic coast connectivity.$$,
      50, 68, 1, 'explorer'),
    ('COG', 'fintech_digital_finance', 'Fintech and Digital Finance',
      $$Congo's financial sector features banking infrastructure, mobile money growth, oil economy finance, CFA franc stability, and financial services serving resource economy.$$,
      $$Republic of Congo has banking sector serving oil economy, government, and urban population. Mobile money adoption is growing. Financial services support oil and gas industry, timber sector, and commerce. CFA franc provides monetary stability. Banking infrastructure is concentrated in Brazzaville and Pointe-Noire. Remittances from Congolese diaspora support households. The sector is positioned for fintech innovation, mobile financial services expansion, digital banking services, and financial inclusion initiatives serving oil-dependent economy, urbanizing population, and French-speaking market in an upper-middle-income, oil-producing country with established banking infrastructure and CFA franc monetary framework.$$,
      55, 68, 2, 'explorer'),
    ('COG', 'energy_renewables', 'Energy and Renewables',
      $$Congo's energy sector combines oil and gas production, hydropower potential, gas-to-power projects, and electrification expansion addressing urban-rural disparities.$$,
      $$Republic of Congo is oil and gas producer with petroleum providing government revenues. The country possesses hydropower potential on Congo River tributaries. Gas utilization for power generation is expanding. Electrification rates are moderate in urban areas but low rurally. Oil sector operations consume substantial energy. Energy infrastructure serves Brazzaville, Pointe-Noire, and oil installations. The sector represents opportunity for hydropower development, gas-to-power expansion, electrification programs, and energy access initiatives supporting economic activity, urban development, and rural energy access in an oil-producing, Congo Basin country with hydropower resources and gas monetization potential.$$,
      58, 70, 3, 'explorer'),
    ('COG', 'agriculture_agribusiness', 'Agriculture and Agribusiness',
      $$Congo's agriculture includes cassava, palm oil, timber, fisheries, and agricultural development supporting food security and economic diversification.$$,
      $$Republic of Congo produces cassava, plantains, vegetables, and palm oil. Timber from Congo Basin forests represents significant export sector. Fisheries operate in Atlantic waters and Congo River. Agriculture supports rural livelihoods. Food security relies substantially on imports despite agricultural potential. Forestry concessions operate under sustainability frameworks. The sector represents opportunity for sustainable forestry, agricultural development, cassava value chains, palm oil production, fisheries development, and food security programs supporting economic diversification, rural livelihoods, and forest conservation in an oil-economy, Congo Basin forest country with timber heritage and agricultural potential.$$,
      48, 65, 4, 'explorer'),
    ('COG', 'mining_critical_minerals', 'Mining and Critical Minerals',
      $$Congo's mining sector includes oil and gas production, potash, iron ore potential, and mineral development supporting diversification beyond petroleum dominance.$$,
      $$Republic of Congo's economy is oil-dependent with offshore production. The country possesses potash deposits and iron ore resources at development stages. Mining sector beyond oil is limited. Geological potential for minerals exists. Oil sector dominates economy but diversification is government priority. Infrastructure and investment constrain mining development. The sector represents mining opportunity for potash development, iron ore projects, oil and gas sector optimization, and mining governance strengthening supporting economic diversification, export revenues, and reduced oil dependency in an oil-producing country pursuing mineral sector diversification.$$,
      52, 65, 5, 'explorer'),
    ('COG', 'logistics_trade', 'Logistics and Trade',
      $$Congo's logistics infrastructure includes Pointe-Noire Atlantic port, Congo-Ocean Railway, Brazzaville river port, and regional corridor positioning.$$,
      $$Republic of Congo operates Pointe-Noire as major Atlantic coast port serving Congolese oil exports, timber exports, and transit trade for landlocked countries. Congo-Ocean Railway connects Pointe-Noire to Brazzaville. Brazzaville river port sits opposite Kinshasa on Congo River. Road networks connect cities and borders. Maya-Maya Airport serves Brazzaville. Oil and timber drive export logistics. The sector represents opportunity for port modernization, rail rehabilitation, river transport development, road infrastructure investment, and regional trade corridor positioning leveraging Atlantic coast, Congo River access, and proximity to Kinshasa serving Congolese oil and timber exports and regional transit potential.$$,
      62, 68, 6, 'explorer'),
    ('COG', 'tourism_hospitality', 'Tourism and Hospitality',
      $$Congo's tourism potential includes lowland gorillas, Odzala National Park, Congo River, rainforest, and eco-tourism addressing infrastructure and conservation challenges.$$,
      $$Republic of Congo offers tourism potential including Odzala-Kokoua National Park with lowland gorillas and forest elephants, Nouabalé-Ndoki National Park, Congo River, and Congo Basin rainforest. Tourism sector is underdeveloped with limited infrastructure. Conservation tourism operates in protected areas. Brazzaville offers urban culture. Air connectivity and hospitality infrastructure are minimal. The sector represents opportunity for gorilla tourism expansion, rainforest eco-tourism, river tourism, conservation tourism, and destination infrastructure supporting economic diversification, conservation financing, and visitor economy development in an oil-economy, Congo Basin forest country with unique wildlife and conservation areas.$$,
      45, 65, 7, 'explorer'),

    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- DR CONGO (COD) — All 7 Sectors
    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ('COD', 'digital_infrastructure', 'Digital Infrastructure',
      $$DR Congo's digital infrastructure is expanding through mobile network growth, Kinshasa fiber development, submarine cable initiatives, and digital services in Africa's largest Francophone market.$$,
      $$Democratic Republic of Congo has growing mobile telecommunications serving Kinshasa megacity and expanding to provinces despite infrastructure challenges. Internet penetration is low but growing. Fiber backbone infrastructure is developing in Kinshasa and major cities. Multiple submarine cable projects aim to provide Atlantic connectivity. E-government services are at early stages. Vast territory and infrastructure deficits create connectivity challenges. The sector represents enormous opportunity for mobile broadband expansion, fiber infrastructure investment, digital public infrastructure, fintech development, and ICT services supporting economic development, mining sector digitalization, and connectivity in Africa's second-largest country and largest Francophone market with vast population and growing digital economy potential.$$,
      48, 75, 1, 'explorer'),
    ('COD', 'fintech_digital_finance', 'Fintech and Digital Finance',
      $$DR Congo's financial sector features high mobile money adoption, limited banking reach, mineral economy finance, and financial innovation addressing access challenges.$$,
      $$DR Congo has very high mobile money adoption with services like M-Pesa, Orange Money, and Airtel Money supporting payments and financial inclusion in context of limited banking infrastructure. Banking sector serves mining industry, government, and urban formal economy. Congolese franc volatility affects financial stability. Remittances from vast Congolese diaspora support households. Financial access is extremely limited in rural areas. The sector demonstrates remarkable mobile financial innovation despite challenges. The sector is positioned for fintech expansion, digital banking services, mining sector financial technology, cross-border payments, and financial inclusion initiatives serving huge, predominantly unbanked population in a vast, mineral-rich, Francophone country with mobile money leadership.$$,
      55, 78, 2, 'explorer'),
    ('COD', 'energy_renewables', 'Energy and Renewables',
      $$DR Congo's energy sector combines enormous hydropower potential, very low access rates, Inga dams, renewable resources, and electrification priorities.$$,
      $$DR Congo possesses among the world's largest hydropower potential with Congo River and tributaries, including Inga dams with potential to power much of Africa. Despite potential, electrification rates are extremely low with limited grid coverage. Diesel and biomass dominate energy use. Infrastructure is severely deficient. Inga rehabilitation and Grand Inga development could transform regional energy. Solar and micro-hydro serve off-grid needs. The sector represents transformational opportunity for Inga hydropower development, regional power trade, solar deployment, mini-grid expansion, and electrification programs supporting economic development, poverty reduction, and regional energy integration in a country with extraordinary hydropower endowment and severe energy deficits.$$,
      42, 80, 3, 'explorer'),
    ('COD', 'agriculture_agribusiness', 'Agriculture and Agribusiness',
      $$DR Congo's agriculture includes cassava, maize, palm oil, coffee, cocoa, vast arable land, and agricultural potential addressing food security and conflict impacts.$$,
      $$DR Congo possesses enormous agricultural potential with vast arable land, favorable climate, and Congo Basin resources. The country produces cassava, maize, rice, palm oil, coffee, and cocoa. Agriculture supports vast majority of population. Conflict, infrastructure deficits, and limited investment constrain productivity. Food security challenges affect eastern regions. Forests provide non-timber products. The sector represents enormous opportunity for agricultural investment, palm oil value chains, coffee and cocoa revival, cassava processing, irrigation, mechanization, and food security programs supporting food self-sufficiency, export potential, and rural livelihoods in a vast, conflict-affected country with extraordinary agricultural endowment.$$,
      50, 70, 4, 'explorer'),
    ('COD', 'mining_critical_minerals', 'Mining and Critical Minerals',
      $$DR Congo's mining sector dominates with cobalt, copper, coltan, diamonds, gold, and critical minerals central to global energy transition and technology supply chains.$$,
      $$DR Congo is world's leading cobalt producer and major copper producer, with vast deposits of coltan (tantalum), tin, tungsten, gold, and diamonds. Katanga and Kasai regions are mining centers. Artisanal mining provides millions of livelihoods. Critical minerals position DR Congo centrally in global energy transition and technology supply chains. Mining governance, conflict minerals, artisanal mining conditions, and revenue transparency are major challenges. Chinese investment dominates sector. The sector represents enormous opportunity and responsibility for responsible mining development, artisanal mining formalization, conflict-free mineral certification, revenue transparency, and mining governance strengthening supporting economic development, state revenues, and global mineral supply in the world's most critical mineral-endowed country.$$,
      75, 75, 5, 'explorer'),
    ('COD', 'logistics_trade', 'Logistics and Trade',
      $$DR Congo's logistics infrastructure features Congo River transport, Matadi Atlantic port, Kinshasa hub, rail corridors, and vast territory logistics addressing infrastructure deficits.$$,
      $$DR Congo operates Matadi as Atlantic coast port with Congo River navigation connecting to Kinshasa and interior. Kinshasa is major inland port opposite Brazzaville. Rail networks include lines to Katanga copper belt and Angola border. Road infrastructure is severely deficient with many areas inaccessible. Air transport serves vast distances. Minerals drive export logistics. Vast territory and infrastructure destruction create enormous logistics challenges. The sector represents opportunity for Congo River transport development, Matadi port expansion, rail rehabilitation, road infrastructure investment, and logistics services supporting mineral exports, regional trade, and internal connectivity in Africa's second-largest country with river transport potential and severe infrastructure rehabilitation needs.$$,
      45, 70, 6, 'explorer'),
    ('COD', 'tourism_hospitality', 'Tourism and Hospitality',
      $$DR Congo's tourism potential includes mountain gorillas, Virunga National Park, Congo Basin rainforest, vast biodiversity, and eco-tourism addressing security and infrastructure.$$,
      $$DR Congo offers extraordinary tourism potential including Virunga National Park with mountain gorillas and active volcanoes, Kahuzi-Biéga National Park with eastern lowland gorillas, Salonga National Park, Congo Basin rainforest, and immense biodiversity. Tourism sector is severely constrained by eastern conflict, infrastructure deficits, and security challenges. Virunga gorilla tourism operates despite challenges. Kinshasa offers urban culture. The sector represents long-term opportunity for gorilla tourism expansion, rainforest eco-tourism, volcano tourism, adventure tourism, and conservation tourism contingent on peace consolidation, security improvement, infrastructure investment, and destination development serving conservation financing and economic diversification in a megadiverse country with unique wildlife.$$,
      48, 65, 7, 'explorer'),

