    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ('GNQ', 'digital_infrastructure', 'Digital Infrastructure',
      $$Equatorial Guinea's digital infrastructure features mobile networks, Malabo and Bata connectivity, submarine cable access, and digital services in Spanish-speaking oil economy.$$,
      $$Equatorial Guinea has mobile telecommunications serving Malabo on Bioko Island, Bata on mainland, and expanding coverage. Submarine cable connectivity provides international bandwidth. Internet access serves urban population and oil sector. E-government services are developing. Small population and oil revenues support infrastructure investment. Spanish and French languages distinguish market. The sector represents opportunity for mobile broadband expansion, digital public services, fintech infrastructure, and technology adoption supporting economic diversification, oil sector operations, and connectivity in a small, oil-rich, Spanish-speaking, island-and-mainland country with hydrocarbon revenues enabling infrastructure investment.$$,
      58, 68, 1, 'explorer'),
    ('GNQ', 'fintech_digital_finance', 'Fintech and Digital Finance',
      $$Equatorial Guinea's financial sector features banking infrastructure, oil economy finance, CFA franc stability, and financial services serving hydrocarbon-driven economy.$$,
      $$Equatorial Guinea has banking sector serving oil and gas industry, government, and small formal economy. Oil revenues generate substantial financial flows. CFA franc provides monetary stability. Banking infrastructure serves Malabo and Bata. Financial services support hydrocarbon sector operations and investments. Mobile money adoption is limited. The sector is positioned for fintech development, digital banking services, and financial diversification supporting economic activity beyond oil in a small, oil-wealthy country with concentrated financial sector, CFA franc framework, and Spanish-speaking market characteristics.$$,
      60, 65, 2, 'explorer'),
    ('GNQ', 'energy_renewables', 'Energy and Renewables',
      $$Equatorial Guinea's energy sector is dominated by oil and gas production with hydropower potential, gas-to-power projects, and energy infrastructure for oil economy.$$,
      $$Equatorial Guinea is major oil and LNG producer with petroleum dominating energy sector and economy. Gas-to-power generation supports electricity supply. The country possesses hydropower potential on mainland rivers. Electrification serves urban areas. Oil and gas revenues fund energy infrastructure. Energy sector is entirely oriented around hydrocarbon production and monetization. The sector represents opportunity for gas-to-power optimization, hydropower development, and energy diversification in a small, oil-and-gas-rich country with hydrocarbon energy abundance and potential for renewable diversification.$$,
      68, 60, 3, 'explorer'),
    ('GNQ', 'agriculture_agribusiness', 'Agriculture and Agribusiness',
      $$Equatorial Guinea's agriculture includes cocoa heritage, cassava, fishing, timber, and agricultural development addressing oil economy concentration.$$,
      $$Equatorial Guinea historically produced cocoa on Bioko Island before oil discovery. Agriculture includes cassava, plantains, and subsistence crops. Timber from mainland rainforests is extracted. Fishing operates in Atlantic waters. Oil wealth has led to agricultural sector neglect. Food security relies heavily on imports. Small population and oil revenues reduce agricultural urgency. The sector represents opportunity for cocoa revival, agricultural development, fisheries expansion, sustainable forestry, and food security initiatives supporting economic diversification and reduced oil dependency in a small, oil-rich country with cocoa heritage and agricultural potential.$$,
      40, 58, 4, 'explorer'),
    ('GNQ', 'mining_critical_minerals', 'Mining and Critical Minerals',
      $$Equatorial Guinea's mining sector is dominated by oil and gas production with limited other mineral activity and geological potential.$$,
      $$Equatorial Guinea's economy is entirely dominated by offshore oil and LNG production. Mineral sector beyond hydrocarbons is minimal. Geological potential for other minerals is largely unexplored. Oil wealth concentration has precluded mineral sector development. The sector represents oil and gas sector optimization and potential geological surveys for mineral diversification in a small, hydrocarbon-dependent economy with limited mining beyond petroleum.$$,
      65, 55, 5, 'explorer'),
    ('GNQ', 'logistics_trade', 'Logistics and Trade',
      $$Equatorial Guinea's logistics infrastructure includes Malabo port on Bioko Island, Bata port on mainland, LNG terminals, and oil export infrastructure.$$,
      $$Equatorial Guinea operates ports at Malabo on Bioko Island and Bata on mainland Río Muni. LNG export terminals serve gas production. Oil export infrastructure dominates logistics. Malabo airport serves international routes. Maritime services support oil and gas sector. Inter-island connectivity links Bioko and mainland. Oil and LNG exports drive logistics activity. The sector represents opportunity for port services, maritime sector development, and regional connectivity in a small, island-and-mainland, oil-exporting country with hydrocarbon logistics infrastructure.$$,
      60, 62, 6, 'explorer'),
    ('GNQ', 'tourism_hospitality', 'Tourism and Hospitality',
      $$Equatorial Guinea's tourism potential includes Bioko Island beaches, Monte Alén National Park, Spanish colonial heritage, and destination development addressing awareness and access.$$,
      $$Equatorial Guinea offers tourism potential including Bioko Island beaches, Monte Alén National Park with rainforest and biodiversity, Spanish colonial architecture in Malabo, and unique Spanish-speaking African culture. Tourism sector is severely underdeveloped with minimal visitors beyond business travelers. Oil wealth has not translated to tourism development. Limited international awareness, difficult visa access, and limited hospitality infrastructure constrain tourism. The sector represents longer-term opportunity for beach tourism, eco-tourism, cultural tourism, and destination development contingent on tourism policy, infrastructure investment, visa liberalization, and destination marketing serving niche tourism and economic diversification in a small, oil-wealthy country with Spanish heritage.$$,
      38, 60, 7, 'explorer'),

