    -- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ('GAB', 'digital_infrastructure', 'Digital Infrastructure',
      $$Gabon's digital infrastructure is well-developed with submarine cable connectivity, fiber networks, Libreville tech hub, and digital services in upper-middle-income oil economy.$$,
      $$Gabon has well-developed telecommunications with submarine cable landings providing international bandwidth. Fiber backbone connects Libreville, Port-Gentil, and major cities. Mobile telecommunications serve urban and expanding rural coverage. Internet access is relatively high for the region. E-government services and digital identity programs are advancing. Libreville has growing tech ecosystem. The sector represents opportunity for digital economy development, fintech infrastructure, cloud services, ICT exports, and technology innovation supporting economic diversification, oil sector digitalization, government efficiency, and positioning as regional digital hub in a French-speaking, upper-middle-income, oil-producing country with quality digital infrastructure.$$,
      68, 70, 1, 'explorer'),
    ('GAB', 'fintech_digital_finance', 'Fintech and Digital Finance',
      $$Gabon's financial sector features developed banking infrastructure, mobile money growth, oil economy finance, CFA franc stability, and financial services serving resource economy.$$,
      $$Gabon has well-developed banking sector serving oil industry, manganese mining, timber sector, and urban population. Mobile money adoption is growing. Financial services are sophisticated by regional standards. CFA franc provides monetary stability. Banking infrastructure serves Libreville and Port-Gentil. Small population and oil wealth support financial sector development. The sector is positioned for fintech innovation, digital banking services, wealth management, and financial diversification in an upper-middle-income, oil-producing country with established banking infrastructure, CFA franc framework, French-speaking market, and relative prosperity enabling financial services development.$$,
      70, 68, 2, 'explorer'),
    ('GAB', 'energy_renewables', 'Energy and Renewables',
      $$Gabon's energy sector combines oil and gas production, hydropower resources, renewable energy potential, and electrification supporting forest conservation goals.$$,
      $$Gabon is oil producer with substantial gas reserves. The country possesses significant hydropower potential on rivers. Electrification rates are relatively high by regional standards. Gas-to-power generation is expanding. Solar energy potential exists. Energy infrastructure serves urban areas and oil/mining operations. Forest conservation commitments influence energy development toward renewables. The sector represents opportunity for hydropower expansion, gas-to-power optimization, solar deployment, and energy transition supporting economic sustainability, forest conservation, and energy access in an oil-producing, forest-rich country pursuing green development pathway with renewable resources.$$,
      65, 68, 3, 'explorer'),
    ('GAB', 'agriculture_agribusiness', 'Agriculture and Agribusiness',
      $$Gabon's agriculture includes palm oil, cassava, timber from sustainable forestry, fisheries, and agricultural development balancing forest conservation with food security.$$,
      $$Gabon produces palm oil, cassava, plantains, and vegetables. Timber from Congo Basin forests represents significant export sector with certified sustainable forestry. Atlantic fisheries operate. Food security relies substantially on imports despite agricultural potential. Rainforest covers most territory with conservation priorities. Small population and oil wealth reduce agricultural urgency but diversification is policy goal. The sector represents opportunity for sustainable agriculture, palm oil value chains, certified timber, fisheries development, and food security programs balancing economic development with forest conservation in an oil-economy, forest-rich country with conservation commitments.$$,
      52, 65, 4, 'explorer'),
    ('GAB', 'mining_critical_minerals', 'Mining and Critical Minerals',
      $$Gabon's mining sector includes oil production, manganese mining, iron ore potential, and mineral development supporting diversification from oil dependence.$$,
      $$Gabon is major manganese producer and oil producer. The country possesses iron ore deposits and other mineral potential. Moanda manganese mines are long-established. Oil sector dominates economy but manganese provides diversification. Mining infrastructure includes Trans-Gabon Railway serving manganese exports. Geological potential for gold and other minerals exists. The sector represents mining opportunity for manganese sector optimization, iron ore development, oil and gas operations, and mineral exploration supporting economic diversification and reduced oil dependency in an upper-middle-income, resource-endowed country pursuing mining sector development alongside forest conservation.$$,
      70, 68, 5, 'explorer'),
    ('GAB', 'logistics_trade', 'Logistics and Trade',
      $$Gabon's logistics infrastructure includes Libreville and Port-Gentil ports, Trans-Gabon Railway, Atlantic coast positioning, and regional trade gateway potential.$$,
      $$Gabon operates ports at Libreville, Port-Gentil, and Owendo serving oil, manganese, and timber exports. Trans-Gabon Railway connects Libreville to Franceville via manganese-mining Moanda. Road networks connect cities and borders. Libreville International Airport serves regional and international routes. Atlantic coast positioning offers logistics potential. Oil, manganese, and timber drive export logistics. The sector represents opportunity for port modernization, rail optimization, road infrastructure investment, and regional logistics hub development leveraging Atlantic coast, railway infrastructure, and positioning for Central African trade serving Gabonese exports and regional transit potential.$$,
      68, 68, 6, 'explorer'),
    ('GAB', 'tourism_hospitality', 'Tourism and Hospitality',
      $$Gabon's tourism showcases pristine rainforest, Loango National Park with beach elephants, surfing hippos, gorillas, and eco-tourism aligned with conservation leadership.$$,
      $$Gabon offers world-class eco-tourism potential including Loango National Park with elephants on beaches and surfing hippos, lowland gorillas, pristine Congo Basin rainforest, Pongara National Park near Libreville, and marine reserves. The country has committed 13 national parks covering 11% of territory. Tourism sector is small but high-value. Air connectivity and hospitality infrastructure are limited but improving. Conservation tourism aligns with national environmental priorities. The sector represents opportunity for luxury eco-tourism expansion, gorilla tourism, beach-rainforest tourism, marine tourism, conservation tourism, and sustainable destination development leveraging extraordinary biodiversity, conservation commitment, political stability, and positioning as premier Central African eco-tourism destination.$$,
      70, 72, 7, 'explorer'),

